# Licence

Two licences, because this repository holds two different kinds of thing.

## The data, the documents and the text: CC BY 4.0

Everything in `demos/` and every README in this repository is licensed under the
**Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International Licence (CC BY 4.0)**.

    Copyright (c) 2026 Learner Labs
    Licensed under CC BY 4.0: https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/

You may share and adapt any of it, including commercially, provided you give credit, link to the
licence, and say whether you changed anything.

That covers the taught documents, the invented company, the invented planet, the two invented
languages, the bulletin examples, the questions, the expected answers, every served answer, and
the session records.

**Why this licence.** The material is entirely ours and entirely synthetic. No customer supplied
it, no third party wrote it, and nothing in it was scraped. It exists so that a result can be
checked, which means the only condition worth attaching is that somebody re-using it says where
it came from. A more restrictive licence would work against the reason the repository exists, and
a licence with no attribution clause would let a corpus of ours circulate with the results
detached from the protocol that produced them, which is how a number becomes a rumour.

**Why not a data-specific licence.** CDLA and ODbL are built for datasets that get merged into
other datasets and need share-alike carried through. Nothing here is a dataset in that sense: it
is a set of documents, questions and answers whose value is that a reader can point at the exact
one we used. CC BY is the licence with the least friction for that, and it is the one an academic
reader already knows how to comply with.

